WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:05.710 --> 00:00:08.030
- Hello, everyone, I'm Saad Omer,

00:00:08.030 --> 00:00:10.030
I'm the director of the Yale Institute

00:00:10.030 --> 00:00:13.020
for Global Health also known as YGH,

00:00:13.020 --> 00:00:16.870
and welcome to the YGH global
health conversation series.

00:00:16.870 --> 00:00:19.730
This is a venue to learn
from, and connect with

00:00:19.730 --> 00:00:21.633
innovative leaders in global health.

00:00:22.610 --> 00:00:24.700
In addition to YGH, we're indebted to

00:00:24.700 --> 00:00:27.090
the George Herbert Walker Jr. Lecture

00:00:27.090 --> 00:00:28.580
at the Macmillan Center at Yale

00:00:28.580 --> 00:00:30.960
for co-sponsoring today's event.

00:00:30.960 --> 00:00:35.570
While we have seen progress,
significant challenges remain

00:00:35.570 --> 00:00:38.380
in our global health response to COVID-19.

00:00:38.380 --> 00:00:41.410
The pandemic continues to
take a devastating toll

00:00:41.410 --> 00:00:43.500
in communities around the world,

00:00:43.500 --> 00:00:48.130
and critically important issues
especially around equity,

00:00:48.130 --> 00:00:51.100
and the availability of vaccines remain.

00:00:51.100 --> 00:00:54.700
So if we were to beat back COVID-19,

00:00:54.700 --> 00:00:58.250
we need to respond as a global community.

00:00:58.250 --> 00:01:00.370
The World Health Organization,
as most of you know

00:01:00.370 --> 00:01:03.520
plays a central role,
if not the central role,

00:01:03.520 --> 00:01:07.390
in marshaling the global response
to the COVID-19 pandemic,

00:01:07.390 --> 00:01:10.830
and many other critical health issues.

00:01:10.830 --> 00:01:14.750
Over the next hour, we will
discuss a range of issues

00:01:14.750 --> 00:01:18.270
including the importance
of ensuring equity

00:01:18.270 --> 00:01:20.330
in tackling the pandemic,

00:01:20.330 --> 00:01:24.600
the value and role of
the WHO in the world,

00:01:24.600 --> 00:01:29.600
and, since we are connecting
from the U.S. at our side,

00:01:31.040 --> 00:01:32.820
the importance of free engaging

00:01:32.820 --> 00:01:35.623
the United States in the global response.

00:01:36.860 --> 00:01:40.780
It is truly an honor for me
to introduce today's guests,

00:01:40.780 --> 00:01:45.500
Dr. Tedros Adhanom Ghebreyesus,

00:01:45.500 --> 00:01:50.150
he was elected the director
general of WHO in May, 2017

00:01:50.150 --> 00:01:53.800
and is the first person
from the WHO African region

00:01:53.800 --> 00:01:57.470
to head the world's leading
public health agency.

00:01:57.470 --> 00:01:59.940
He has been a pioneer in global health,

00:01:59.940 --> 00:02:03.790
well before his start with
WHO, as the director general,

00:02:03.790 --> 00:02:06.400
he was born in Eritrean city of Asmara.

00:02:06.400 --> 00:02:08.830
Dr. Tedros graduated from
the University of Asmara

00:02:08.830 --> 00:02:11.460
with a Bachelor of Biology before earning

00:02:11.460 --> 00:02:14.940
a Master of Science in
Immunology of Infectious Diseases

00:02:14.940 --> 00:02:18.750
from the University of London,
a PhD in community health

00:02:18.750 --> 00:02:20.590
from the University of Nottingham

00:02:20.590 --> 00:02:22.790
and an Honorary Fellowship
from the London School

00:02:22.790 --> 00:02:25.140
of Hygiene and Tropical Medicine.

00:02:25.140 --> 00:02:29.340
Following his studies, Dr.
Tedros returned to Ethiopia

00:02:29.340 --> 00:02:32.230
to support the delivery of health services

00:02:32.230 --> 00:02:34.610
and doing some pioneering work

00:02:34.610 --> 00:02:37.073
starting as a field level, malariologist,

00:02:38.490 --> 00:02:40.860
before heading a regional health service

00:02:40.860 --> 00:02:43.740
and later serving in
Ethiopia's federal government

00:02:43.740 --> 00:02:46.550
for over a decade as minister of health

00:02:46.550 --> 00:02:48.480
and minister of foreign affairs.

00:02:48.480 --> 00:02:50.440
Under his leadership Ethiopia expanded

00:02:50.440 --> 00:02:52.070
its health infrastructure,

00:02:52.070 --> 00:02:55.520
developed innovative health
financing mechanisms,

00:02:55.520 --> 00:02:58.990
and expanded its health workforce.

00:02:58.990 --> 00:03:02.780
A particular, a major
component of reforms he drove,

00:03:02.780 --> 00:03:07.370
was the creation of a primary
healthcare extension program

00:03:07.370 --> 00:03:10.170
that deployed approximately 40,000

00:03:10.170 --> 00:03:12.710
female health workers
throughout the country.

00:03:12.710 --> 00:03:16.310
A significant result, was
an approximate 60% reduction

00:03:16.310 --> 00:03:18.410
in child and maternal mortality,

00:03:18.410 --> 00:03:20.503
compared to the 2,000 level.

00:03:21.920 --> 00:03:26.920
So as minister of foreign
affairs from 2012 to 2016

00:03:26.930 --> 00:03:29.693
he elevated health as a profession,

00:03:31.180 --> 00:03:34.010
health as a political issue, nationally,

00:03:34.010 --> 00:03:36.100
regionally as well as globally,

00:03:36.100 --> 00:03:37.980
and in this role he led efforts

00:03:37.980 --> 00:03:40.980
to negotiate Addis Ababa action agenda

00:03:40.980 --> 00:03:44.230
in which now 193 countries committed

00:03:44.230 --> 00:03:47.750
to the financing necessary to achieve

00:03:47.750 --> 00:03:49.600
sustainable development goals.

00:03:49.600 --> 00:03:51.854
Prior to his election as the DG for WHO

00:03:51.854 --> 00:03:54.790
Dr. Tedros held many leadership
positions in global health,

00:03:54.790 --> 00:03:57.560
including the chair of
the Global Health Fund

00:03:59.442 --> 00:04:02.610
to fight AIDS, tuberculosis, and malaria,

00:04:02.610 --> 00:04:05.850
chair of the Rollback Malaria Partnership,

00:04:05.850 --> 00:04:07.310
and co-chair of the Partnership

00:04:07.310 --> 00:04:09.900
for Maternal Newborn and Childhood Board.

00:04:09.900 --> 00:04:14.190
After taking office as
the WHO director general,

00:04:14.190 --> 00:04:18.220
in 2017 he initiated the most significant

00:04:18.220 --> 00:04:22.360
organizational transformation
in that organization's history

00:04:22.360 --> 00:04:25.840
which has generated a wide
range of achievements.

00:04:25.840 --> 00:04:27.770
Welcome Dr. Tedros, we're so pleased

00:04:27.770 --> 00:04:29.430
to have you here with us today,

00:04:29.430 --> 00:04:32.030
and would love to start
with a few remarks from you.

00:04:33.720 --> 00:04:37.430
- Thank you, thank you so
much Saad, can you hear me?

00:04:37.430 --> 00:04:39.613
- Absolutely, loud and clear.

00:04:39.613 --> 00:04:41.263
- Thank you, thank you my friend.

00:04:43.140 --> 00:04:45.880
First of all, I'd like to
say I just started earlier

00:04:45.880 --> 00:04:49.870
telling you, I have a very strong link

00:04:49.870 --> 00:04:52.770
with Yale University.

00:04:52.770 --> 00:04:56.680
That started when I was
minister of health in Ethiopia.

00:04:56.680 --> 00:05:01.050
And the bridge was your own director

00:05:01.050 --> 00:05:04.050
of global health at the time,

00:05:04.050 --> 00:05:07.103
now she is the president
of Vassar College,

00:05:08.210 --> 00:05:11.763
Dr. Elizabeth Bradley, I call her Betsy.

00:05:12.970 --> 00:05:17.550
So we had a partnership, Yale University,

00:05:17.550 --> 00:05:22.550
Clinton Foundation, and
Ethiopian Ministry of Health

00:05:22.590 --> 00:05:24.023
on hospital reform.

00:05:25.340 --> 00:05:27.623
But it didn't just stop there,

00:05:28.820 --> 00:05:31.680
we cooperated on hospital reform,

00:05:31.680 --> 00:05:36.130
and we had one of the biggest
reforms in Ethiopia together

00:05:36.130 --> 00:05:39.730
the three of us, ministry
of health of Ethiopia,

00:05:39.730 --> 00:05:44.090
Yale University and Clinton foundation.

00:05:44.090 --> 00:05:46.510
And that was followed actually,

00:05:46.510 --> 00:05:48.963
when I became foreign minister,

00:05:49.840 --> 00:05:52.040
we continued the partnership

00:05:52.040 --> 00:05:56.500
and we used to have
trainings organized by Yale,

00:05:56.500 --> 00:06:00.860
you know, John Lewis
Gaddis on grand strategy

00:06:00.860 --> 00:06:04.233
and training our diplomats
on grand strategy.

00:06:05.450 --> 00:06:07.600
Of course, we use grand strategy also

00:06:07.600 --> 00:06:11.560
for our reform in Ethiopia

00:06:11.560 --> 00:06:15.963
with the support from
Betsy and John Gaddis.

00:06:18.057 --> 00:06:22.320
And so I will stop there,
but I have many, many stories

00:06:22.320 --> 00:06:25.720
I could have told you linked to Yale.

00:06:25.720 --> 00:06:27.810
But I would like to thank Betsy

00:06:27.810 --> 00:06:31.900
who has been the center, a
force of nature actually,

00:06:31.900 --> 00:06:36.900
who helped in reforming the
health sector in Ethiopia.

00:06:42.110 --> 00:06:45.960
But not only Betsy, I have many friends

00:06:45.960 --> 00:06:50.960
and I would like to say, hello,
and greetings to all of you,

00:06:51.580 --> 00:06:54.160
and I hope this pandemic
will be behind us,

00:06:54.160 --> 00:06:59.160
and I will have a chance to
come to visit you at Yale.

00:06:59.860 --> 00:07:04.860
And of course my sister, Betsy in Vassar,

00:07:05.080 --> 00:07:07.536
I don't know if she's listening to this,

00:07:07.536 --> 00:07:08.770
(laughing)

00:07:08.770 --> 00:07:12.083
once Yale always Yale, so she might be.

00:07:12.083 --> 00:07:13.870
(laughing)

00:07:13.870 --> 00:07:18.870
Then greetings also to the
members and the students

00:07:19.230 --> 00:07:22.280
of the Yale Institute for Global Health,

00:07:22.280 --> 00:07:26.080
and to all the people
following this event online.

00:07:26.080 --> 00:07:29.630
And thank you for the
opportunity to be with you today

00:07:29.630 --> 00:07:32.710
and to discuss the critical
need for equitable access

00:07:32.710 --> 00:07:34.880
to health services worldwide,

00:07:34.880 --> 00:07:39.620
and the essential steps we
must take to achieve this.

00:07:39.620 --> 00:07:42.470
First, I would like to
give a special thanks

00:07:42.470 --> 00:07:44.960
to our moderator, my friend Saad Omer,

00:07:46.270 --> 00:07:48.630
for his kind words as well as the support

00:07:48.630 --> 00:07:51.420
he's providing WHO and the world

00:07:51.420 --> 00:07:54.970
as a member of the WHO
technical advisory group

00:07:54.970 --> 00:07:58.030
we call TAG, on behavioral insights

00:07:58.030 --> 00:08:00.520
which is actually a new addition

00:08:00.520 --> 00:08:02.867
and part of the transformation in WHO.

00:08:04.610 --> 00:08:07.830
This independent expert
group is advising WHO

00:08:07.830 --> 00:08:10.480
on understanding the many factors

00:08:10.480 --> 00:08:14.050
that affect people
behaviors and practices,

00:08:14.050 --> 00:08:16.660
which is vital for helping countries

00:08:16.660 --> 00:08:21.270
design more equitable health
policies and programs.

00:08:21.270 --> 00:08:23.740
Of course, the chair is professor Cass,

00:08:23.740 --> 00:08:26.650
and they were working
with Saad very closely

00:08:26.650 --> 00:08:29.930
professor Cass from Harvard.

00:08:29.930 --> 00:08:32.930
This is the type of work that's critical

00:08:32.930 --> 00:08:37.040
to WHO's efforts to
advance health for all.

00:08:37.040 --> 00:08:40.070
Which includes not only
universal health coverage,

00:08:40.070 --> 00:08:43.380
but also the social, environmental,

00:08:43.380 --> 00:08:47.920
and economic policies and
factors that affect health.

00:08:47.920 --> 00:08:52.920
As COVID-19 has harshly
illustrated equity matters.

00:08:54.580 --> 00:08:56.560
This pandemic has thrived

00:08:56.560 --> 00:09:00.780
amid the inequalities and
inequities in our societies

00:09:00.780 --> 00:09:05.330
and exploited the gaps
in our health systems.

00:09:05.330 --> 00:09:07.900
It has exacerbated the disparities

00:09:07.900 --> 00:09:11.300
between and within countries.

00:09:11.300 --> 00:09:14.810
But while the pandemic
has affected all of us,

00:09:14.810 --> 00:09:19.730
the poorest and most marginalized
have been hit hardest.

00:09:19.730 --> 00:09:23.603
Both in terms of lives
and livelihoods lost.

00:09:24.540 --> 00:09:27.460
The impact of the COVID-19 crisis

00:09:27.460 --> 00:09:30.880
pushed an estimated 120 million people

00:09:30.880 --> 00:09:33.573
into extreme poverty last year.

00:09:34.430 --> 00:09:38.180
Gender inequalities have
significantly increased

00:09:38.180 --> 00:09:42.073
with more women than men
leaving the labor force.

00:09:42.960 --> 00:09:46.150
And as we speak, rich
countries are vaccinating

00:09:46.150 --> 00:09:51.123
their populations while the
world's poor watch and wait.

00:09:52.690 --> 00:09:56.120
As you may know high and
upper-middle income countries,

00:09:56.120 --> 00:10:00.260
which account for 53% of
the world's population,

00:10:00.260 --> 00:10:03.863
have administered 83%
of the world's vaccines.

00:10:05.110 --> 00:10:08.900
Meanwhile, low and lower
middle income countries

00:10:08.900 --> 00:10:12.630
which make up 47% of the world's people,

00:10:12.630 --> 00:10:16.693
have received just 17%
of the world's vaccines.

00:10:18.410 --> 00:10:23.410
The inequitable distribution
of vaccines is a moral outrage.

00:10:23.440 --> 00:10:28.440
It's also economically and
epidemiologically self-defeating.

00:10:29.840 --> 00:10:31.940
As COVID-19 has shown,

00:10:31.940 --> 00:10:35.103
the pandemic is more than a health crisis.

00:10:36.430 --> 00:10:40.370
It has had grave
consequences for livelihoods,

00:10:40.370 --> 00:10:42.203
businesses, and economies.

00:10:43.530 --> 00:10:46.893
The pandemic has shown that
when health is at risk,

00:10:47.880 --> 00:10:49.253
everything is at risk.

00:10:50.260 --> 00:10:54.410
But when health is protected and promoted,

00:10:54.410 --> 00:10:58.020
individuals, families, communities,

00:10:58.020 --> 00:11:00.803
economies and nations can thrive.

00:11:02.840 --> 00:11:06.770
Health should not be seen as a cost,

00:11:06.770 --> 00:11:11.260
but as an investment in
productive, resilient

00:11:11.260 --> 00:11:13.223
and inclusive societies.

00:11:14.390 --> 00:11:18.900
But globally, as you're observing,

00:11:18.900 --> 00:11:21.943
we're going in the wrong direction.

00:11:23.680 --> 00:11:26.940
Around the world, more
households are spending

00:11:26.940 --> 00:11:30.323
an increasing proportion
of their income on health.

00:11:31.740 --> 00:11:35.150
And more people are
being exposed to poverty

00:11:35.150 --> 00:11:38.863
as a result of out of
pocket health spending.

00:11:40.210 --> 00:11:42.010
It's clear that making progress

00:11:42.010 --> 00:11:44.210
toward this universal health coverage,

00:11:44.210 --> 00:11:48.860
cannot just be a matter
for ministries of health,

00:11:48.860 --> 00:11:52.590
but takes an all of government approach

00:11:52.590 --> 00:11:57.063
with leadership from the
highest political levels.

00:11:58.390 --> 00:12:02.670
At the United Nations
general assembly in 2019,

00:12:02.670 --> 00:12:06.400
just a few months before
the pandemic started,

00:12:06.400 --> 00:12:09.170
UN member states endorsed the high level

00:12:09.170 --> 00:12:12.593
political declaration on
universal health coverage.

00:12:14.240 --> 00:12:17.960
We must learn the
lessons of this pandemic.

00:12:17.960 --> 00:12:21.420
and build on the political
momentum for equity

00:12:21.420 --> 00:12:24.700
to mobilize investments for healthier,

00:12:24.700 --> 00:12:28.563
more resilient, and more
sustainable societies.

00:12:29.510 --> 00:12:33.900
In closing, I would like to
offer a few things to consider,

00:12:33.900 --> 00:12:36.350
as you move forward in your studies,

00:12:36.350 --> 00:12:39.350
and your careers in global health.

00:12:39.350 --> 00:12:44.350
First, remember that health
is part of a larger ecosystem

00:12:44.990 --> 00:12:47.780
that encompasses environmental,

00:12:47.780 --> 00:12:52.483
social, economic and political factors.

00:12:54.250 --> 00:12:58.490
That's why it's important
for health professionals

00:12:58.490 --> 00:13:03.490
and policy makers to take
a multi-sectoral approach.

00:13:03.830 --> 00:13:06.903
Don't be afraid to work
across disciplines.

00:13:08.840 --> 00:13:12.020
Partnerships can make us stronger

00:13:12.020 --> 00:13:14.003
and achieve the desired result.

00:13:15.350 --> 00:13:20.350
Second, search for examples
of what works in countries,

00:13:20.740 --> 00:13:22.713
and communities around the world.

00:13:24.120 --> 00:13:28.470
Reverse engineer them and
see what might inspire

00:13:28.470 --> 00:13:32.033
further research leading
to tangible global change.

00:13:33.550 --> 00:13:36.123
And best practices can
be found everywhere.

00:13:37.080 --> 00:13:39.840
In high-income countries,
low-income countries,

00:13:39.840 --> 00:13:41.223
middle income countries.

00:13:42.210 --> 00:13:45.170
And the pandemic has shown us that.

00:13:45.170 --> 00:13:50.170
Even the countries, the
high-income countries

00:13:50.470 --> 00:13:53.563
were very vulnerable.

00:13:58.660 --> 00:14:03.660
And third, let's rethink
that narrative around health.

00:14:06.040 --> 00:14:11.040
Not as a cost, but as an
investment in our common future.

00:14:13.430 --> 00:14:17.880
Strong health systems and
global health security,

00:14:17.880 --> 00:14:20.363
are two sides of the same coin.

00:14:21.730 --> 00:14:25.280
Now, let's get started
with our discussion,

00:14:25.280 --> 00:14:27.940
I'm looking forward to your questions.

00:14:27.940 --> 00:14:32.940
Thank you again, Professor
Saad Omer, and back to you.

00:14:35.760 --> 00:14:38.630
- Thank you, and thanks for
the kind words about Yale

00:14:38.630 --> 00:14:40.960
and your previous
interaction and collaboration

00:14:40.960 --> 00:14:43.970
and work with this institution.

00:14:43.970 --> 00:14:46.170
You know, since you
mentioned Betsy Bradley,

00:14:47.440 --> 00:14:52.440
she and I had a chat a few
weeks ago around an event,

00:14:52.910 --> 00:14:54.770
and she was very much planning to attend

00:14:54.770 --> 00:14:58.810
and she's likely to be
listening in and streaming this.

00:14:58.810 --> 00:15:03.533
So rest assured one of your
old friends is very much here,

00:15:04.780 --> 00:15:07.690
is likely to be here,
I haven't confirmed it,

00:15:07.690 --> 00:15:09.560
but she was intending to attend.

00:15:09.560 --> 00:15:12.870
So I think, you know, thanks
for laying out this vision

00:15:12.870 --> 00:15:16.140
for an equitable future in health.

00:15:16.140 --> 00:15:20.400
But focusing on the more short term,

00:15:20.400 --> 00:15:24.820
how do we as a global
community work towards

00:15:24.820 --> 00:15:27.010
equitable access to vaccines, treatments,

00:15:27.010 --> 00:15:29.443
and other health services
in this pandemic?

00:15:35.810 --> 00:15:38.560
- No, thank you, thank you very much.

00:15:38.560 --> 00:15:42.840
I think what's key in
especially ensuring equitable

00:15:42.840 --> 00:15:47.763
access is political commitment.

00:15:50.010 --> 00:15:53.760
And that political commitment
should actually emanate

00:15:53.760 --> 00:15:56.790
from a belief that sharing or cooperation,

00:16:00.860 --> 00:16:03.500
sharing of vaccine, that's cooperation,

00:16:03.500 --> 00:16:07.933
is in the interest of
every individual country.

00:16:09.220 --> 00:16:13.890
So any country you name
should believe that

00:16:13.890 --> 00:16:18.890
the vaccine equity is
good to end this pandemic,

00:16:19.680 --> 00:16:22.270
and it's in the interest of all of us

00:16:22.270 --> 00:16:24.853
or in the interest of every nation.

00:16:26.380 --> 00:16:28.723
I can give you one example for instance,

00:16:29.890 --> 00:16:33.097
you know, new variants are appearing.

00:16:34.200 --> 00:16:36.570
I know many countries are moving

00:16:36.570 --> 00:16:39.710
especially high income
countries are moving

00:16:39.710 --> 00:16:43.203
with high vaccination coverage.

00:16:45.130 --> 00:16:48.470
Some of them may achieve 70 or 80%

00:16:48.470 --> 00:16:51.083
by summer or end of this year.

00:16:52.710 --> 00:16:56.453
But if the rest of the
world remains unvaccinated

00:16:58.370 --> 00:17:01.580
or low coverage like what we see now, 0.3%

00:17:01.580 --> 00:17:03.890
in low-income countries,

00:17:03.890 --> 00:17:08.890
then the new variants
will continue to mutate,

00:17:09.060 --> 00:17:13.980
and then you may find
variance that may actually

00:17:13.980 --> 00:17:18.733
evade the vaccines that
are already being used.

00:17:19.780 --> 00:17:23.270
Then those countries who believe

00:17:23.270 --> 00:17:26.190
that they have already high
coverage will be affected

00:17:26.190 --> 00:17:30.252
because there is a new guy, a new virus,

00:17:30.252 --> 00:17:33.250
mutated significantly means a new one,

00:17:33.250 --> 00:17:38.250
it's almost like new, that
will again affect them.

00:17:38.820 --> 00:17:43.413
So they are doing it
for their own interests

00:17:45.240 --> 00:17:49.020
and I think that understanding will help

00:17:49.020 --> 00:17:51.573
in vaccine equity nothing
else, to be honest.

00:17:53.090 --> 00:17:57.000
And the second, maybe
to see the other way,

00:17:57.000 --> 00:17:58.403
this is a common enemy.

00:17:59.640 --> 00:18:02.720
And unless we fight it together,

00:18:02.720 --> 00:18:07.720
unless we fight it in unison,
it will not be defeated.

00:18:10.610 --> 00:18:11.980
And if we are divided

00:18:11.980 --> 00:18:14.483
it will use the divisions to attack us.

00:18:15.610 --> 00:18:19.810
So if we're divided,
we're more vulnerable.

00:18:19.810 --> 00:18:23.420
If we work in unison, then we will be able

00:18:23.420 --> 00:18:27.060
to defeat this virus and end the pandemic

00:18:27.060 --> 00:18:29.470
as soon as possible.

00:18:29.470 --> 00:18:33.790
So it's a must, actually the
only way to end this pandemic

00:18:33.790 --> 00:18:35.093
is through cooperation.

00:18:36.810 --> 00:18:41.660
So that's it, it's really
realizing this issue

00:18:41.660 --> 00:18:45.070
that will, I mean, realizing
this, that will help us.

00:18:45.070 --> 00:18:48.640
And then of course, I understand
there are some concerns,

00:18:48.640 --> 00:18:52.910
for instance, the private sector.

00:18:52.910 --> 00:18:57.203
I think we need to put
people before profits.

00:19:00.110 --> 00:19:02.913
And we have to make a choice.

00:19:03.830 --> 00:19:06.460
Because making profits
will be at the expense

00:19:06.460 --> 00:19:10.590
of losing many lives and at the expense

00:19:10.590 --> 00:19:15.590
of extending the pandemic.

00:19:17.210 --> 00:19:22.210
So, I mean, it's very clear
for those who want to act.

00:19:23.210 --> 00:19:26.150
And then of course, if there
is political commitment

00:19:26.150 --> 00:19:28.890
then you move into increasing

00:19:28.890 --> 00:19:33.250
production capacity by waving IP.

00:19:33.250 --> 00:19:36.180
For instance, if you
believe that, you know

00:19:36.180 --> 00:19:38.360
people come first before profit,

00:19:38.360 --> 00:19:42.210
then you waive IP and
you take other measures

00:19:42.210 --> 00:19:46.540
like technology transfer and
so on to increase production,

00:19:46.540 --> 00:19:49.870
have enough supplies to share.

00:19:49.870 --> 00:19:52.920
But to make that happen
you will need also money.

00:19:52.920 --> 00:19:54.870
So you need to mobilize resources.

00:19:54.870 --> 00:19:57.280
As we speak Now we need
19 billion U.S. dollars

00:19:57.280 --> 00:20:02.280
for ACT Accelerator, but
we are not getting it.

00:20:03.880 --> 00:20:07.330
Of course, U.S. has contributed
4 billion U.S. dollars

00:20:07.330 --> 00:20:09.963
but still there is a gap.

00:20:11.410 --> 00:20:13.580
But as you know many high income countries

00:20:13.580 --> 00:20:15.180
are already spending a lot of money

00:20:15.180 --> 00:20:16.773
for their stimulus package.

00:20:18.300 --> 00:20:21.020
19 billion compared to
the stimulus package

00:20:21.020 --> 00:20:23.103
is a very small amount of money.

00:20:24.000 --> 00:20:25.660
So if there is real commitment

00:20:25.660 --> 00:20:27.890
which I say the political commitment,

00:20:27.890 --> 00:20:31.040
then the financing should also come

00:20:31.040 --> 00:20:33.910
because the increased production capacity

00:20:33.910 --> 00:20:36.400
cannot come without financing.

00:20:36.400 --> 00:20:39.390
So if we do political commitment

00:20:40.580 --> 00:20:42.130
and that means we'll be followed

00:20:42.130 --> 00:20:46.750
by taking every action
to increase production

00:20:46.750 --> 00:20:50.070
and then to increase production
we will need financing

00:20:50.070 --> 00:20:54.500
if we do those and if
we identify this virus

00:20:54.500 --> 00:20:59.190
as a common enemy and it's
in the interest of all of us

00:20:59.190 --> 00:21:03.800
I think the vaccine equity can be done.

00:21:03.800 --> 00:21:05.843
Thank you and back to you.

00:21:06.880 --> 00:21:09.490
- No, I think thanks for
highlighting the value

00:21:09.490 --> 00:21:12.560
of global engagement with WHO.

00:21:12.560 --> 00:21:16.880
Switching to the US, what is
that partnership look like

00:21:16.880 --> 00:21:20.670
moving forward, and then what explicitly

00:21:20.670 --> 00:21:22.250
is needed from your perspective

00:21:22.250 --> 00:21:24.290
from the Biden administration

00:21:24.290 --> 00:21:27.963
to fulfill the promise of WHO
and the multilateral system?

00:21:29.390 --> 00:21:31.813
- Yeah, thank you so much.

00:21:31.813 --> 00:21:34.530
First of all, I would like to start

00:21:34.530 --> 00:21:37.743
by thanking the Biden administration.

00:21:39.310 --> 00:21:42.350
As you may remember, during
the election campaign,

00:21:42.350 --> 00:21:47.350
he promised to reverse the
former administration's decision

00:21:49.560 --> 00:21:52.230
that they, office inauguration.

00:21:52.230 --> 00:21:56.150
So he signed a letter
actually on January 20th

00:21:57.160 --> 00:22:00.113
that reverses the decision.

00:22:01.590 --> 00:22:03.450
So he kept his promise.

00:22:03.450 --> 00:22:08.450
And second, I had a call on 21st January

00:22:09.340 --> 00:22:10.710
the day I received the letter

00:22:10.710 --> 00:22:13.600
from his excellency the president,

00:22:13.600 --> 00:22:16.640
I received a call from vice
president Kamala Harris

00:22:17.780 --> 00:22:22.780
and, you know on U.S.
commitment to work with WHO

00:22:23.260 --> 00:22:27.280
and to help global health.

00:22:27.280 --> 00:22:32.280
And then, third, I had a call
with the same day actually

00:22:34.755 --> 00:22:38.940
there was a presentation
to our board by Tony Fauci.

00:22:38.940 --> 00:22:40.570
And then of course I had a call

00:22:40.570 --> 00:22:43.560
after the secretary of
health was appointed

00:22:45.975 --> 00:22:49.363
with secretary Xavier Becerra,.

00:22:50.560 --> 00:22:53.840
You can see the strong commitment

00:22:53.840 --> 00:22:56.920
and I have to really appreciate that.

00:22:56.920 --> 00:23:01.920
And what I expect is what
the Biden administration

00:23:01.930 --> 00:23:02.793
is saying now.

00:23:04.410 --> 00:23:09.410
For the US to be in WHO
is good for the U.S.,

00:23:11.270 --> 00:23:14.990
good for the U.S., and
good for the U.S. citizens

00:23:14.990 --> 00:23:18.463
and good for the rest of
the world, and good for WHO.

00:23:20.270 --> 00:23:24.260
So, joining WHO, working with WHO

00:23:24.260 --> 00:23:26.240
is in the interest of the U.S.

00:23:26.240 --> 00:23:28.160
Biden administration believes this

00:23:28.160 --> 00:23:30.260
and we believe that by the way.

00:23:30.260 --> 00:23:33.960
And it's in the interest
of the rest of the world.

00:23:33.960 --> 00:23:38.053
That mentality is important
because it's the mindset

00:23:40.460 --> 00:23:43.730
that leads you to effective partnership.

00:23:43.730 --> 00:23:47.640
So the mindset of this
administration is, U.S. benefits,

00:23:47.640 --> 00:23:51.630
the rest of the world benefits
if the U.S. engages WHO.

00:23:51.630 --> 00:23:54.580
That's very important, and that's why

00:23:54.580 --> 00:23:56.220
they're doing what they're doing now

00:23:56.220 --> 00:23:58.383
and which we were
expecting, joining COVAX,

00:23:58.383 --> 00:24:03.383
supporting COVAX with
4 billion U.S. dollars,

00:24:04.440 --> 00:24:09.280
and also, you know, activating
all the program support

00:24:09.280 --> 00:24:13.420
they have been providing
us, the financial support,

00:24:13.420 --> 00:24:17.260
and working with us very
closely, not only to fight COVID,

00:24:17.260 --> 00:24:20.950
but to fight other diseases as well.

00:24:20.950 --> 00:24:25.950
Yeah, so this is what
we have been expecting,

00:24:27.450 --> 00:24:31.520
and we see that that's happening

00:24:31.520 --> 00:24:35.330
and we will continue to work with the U.S.

00:24:35.330 --> 00:24:40.330
very, very closely and, you
know have better outcome

00:24:41.180 --> 00:24:43.810
in the fight against this pandemic,

00:24:43.810 --> 00:24:48.253
but at the same time
in other programs, too.

00:24:50.440 --> 00:24:51.400
- Wonderful.

00:24:51.400 --> 00:24:54.350
So, you know, more than
two dozen countries

00:24:54.350 --> 00:24:57.420
and the European Council
president have signed a statement

00:24:57.420 --> 00:25:02.050
calling for the creation of
a new international treaty

00:25:02.050 --> 00:25:04.260
for pandemic preparedness and response.

00:25:04.260 --> 00:25:06.630
What should be the key
elements of this treaty

00:25:06.630 --> 00:25:08.900
and your overall perspective on the treaty

00:25:08.900 --> 00:25:11.673
and on what should be the WHO's role?

00:25:12.820 --> 00:25:14.300
- Yeah, thank you.

00:25:14.300 --> 00:25:19.300
As you know, before the
pandemic many people,

00:25:21.120 --> 00:25:25.100
many institutions,
including WHO are saying

00:25:25.100 --> 00:25:28.663
the world is unprepared for a pandemic.

00:25:32.210 --> 00:25:35.343
And what we see now is exactly that.

00:25:36.580 --> 00:25:39.010
And not the only we were saying
the world it is unprepared

00:25:39.010 --> 00:25:42.453
but we weren't proposing
solutions or actions,

00:25:44.340 --> 00:25:46.860
but I think action was late,

00:25:46.860 --> 00:25:50.263
and that's why the pandemic
is now wrecking havoc.

00:25:51.673 --> 00:25:56.673
And we now know, how unprepared we were,

00:26:00.540 --> 00:26:03.903
and we now know what
the major problems were,

00:26:06.730 --> 00:26:07.680
more than ever before.

00:26:07.680 --> 00:26:12.210
Of course we knew them,
but even the hardest way

00:26:12.210 --> 00:26:13.850
we know now.

00:26:13.850 --> 00:26:16.890
And the problems are
poor national capacity

00:26:16.890 --> 00:26:19.790
in emergency preparedness and response.

00:26:19.790 --> 00:26:23.423
And at the same time,
poor global capacity.

00:26:24.360 --> 00:26:29.360
When there is poor, or
weak national capacity,

00:26:29.600 --> 00:26:32.303
it makes us vulnerable as a globe.

00:26:34.040 --> 00:26:39.040
And our strength is as
strong as the weakest link.

00:26:39.280 --> 00:26:42.913
Or we are globally as
weak as the weakest link.

00:26:44.010 --> 00:26:49.010
Unless we address the
weaknesses that we have.

00:26:49.040 --> 00:26:51.640
So this is very clear now,

00:26:51.640 --> 00:26:53.110
and the investment should stop

00:26:53.110 --> 00:26:56.813
from building national capacity,

00:26:57.760 --> 00:27:00.003
and the pandemic treaty can help.

00:27:01.540 --> 00:27:05.910
Maybe if I go to specific
other challenges problems

00:27:07.020 --> 00:27:10.710
that are common factor,
be it as national capacity

00:27:10.710 --> 00:27:13.810
or global capacities, for instance,

00:27:13.810 --> 00:27:17.140
we have seen weaknesses in sharing better.

00:27:17.140 --> 00:27:18.680
Very serious problem.

00:27:19.862 --> 00:27:23.210
There are problems in sharing pathogens

00:27:23.210 --> 00:27:24.773
or biological material.

00:27:25.960 --> 00:27:30.690
And there is a problem in
sharing technology like vaccines,

00:27:30.690 --> 00:27:33.050
we just, we have been discussing,

00:27:33.050 --> 00:27:37.180
and there is a problem in
sharing resources like financing.

00:27:37.180 --> 00:27:40.623
Financing, preparedness and response.

00:27:41.470 --> 00:27:46.120
So we believe that the pandemic treaty

00:27:46.120 --> 00:27:50.243
which we are now pushing
for, can address this gaps.

00:27:52.990 --> 00:27:56.710
Without sharing information,
you can not fight

00:27:56.710 --> 00:27:58.833
an outbreak, epidemic or pandemic.

00:27:59.820 --> 00:28:02.010
That has to be addressed

00:28:02.010 --> 00:28:04.990
without sharing the pathogens
and if there is a problem

00:28:04.990 --> 00:28:07.667
in sharing pathogens and you know,

00:28:07.667 --> 00:28:10.150
all the problems associated with that.

00:28:10.150 --> 00:28:14.680
And without sharing vaccines,
now you see the disparity

00:28:14.680 --> 00:28:17.920
and the virus is continuing to wreck havoc

00:28:17.920 --> 00:28:19.580
throughout the world.

00:28:19.580 --> 00:28:22.590
So we believe the pandemic
treaty can address

00:28:22.590 --> 00:28:27.260
this specific weaknesses
among other things.

00:28:27.260 --> 00:28:29.930
Of course, the pandemic
treaty is not all about

00:28:29.930 --> 00:28:32.070
these five things I raised.

00:28:32.070 --> 00:28:34.120
It's more than that, but these are

00:28:34.120 --> 00:28:36.040
some of the critical ones.

00:28:36.040 --> 00:28:41.040
So national capacity, global
capacity, sharing information,

00:28:41.430 --> 00:28:44.440
sharing pathogens for biological material,

00:28:44.440 --> 00:28:47.350
sharing technology like vaccines,

00:28:47.350 --> 00:28:50.300
and sharing resources and strings

00:28:50.300 --> 00:28:54.140
and the whole global
preparedness and response.

00:28:54.140 --> 00:28:57.510
That's what the pandemic treaty can do.

00:28:57.510 --> 00:29:00.880
And there is an opportunity
now because of the pandemic

00:29:00.880 --> 00:29:05.510
and let's use this
pandemic as an opportunity.

00:29:05.510 --> 00:29:10.510
Like as the saying goes, use
every crisis as an opportunity.

00:29:11.080 --> 00:29:13.920
If we're going to use this
crisis as an opportunity,

00:29:13.920 --> 00:29:17.920
then the solution, pandemic
treaty that addresses

00:29:17.920 --> 00:29:21.510
the major weaknesses we
had, is very important,

00:29:21.510 --> 00:29:23.410
and something that we can

00:29:23.410 --> 00:29:25.713
pass to our children and grandchildren.

00:29:28.810 --> 00:29:31.470
- So before the pandemic,
right before the pandemic

00:29:31.470 --> 00:29:34.530
and if I can sort of
editorialize a little bit,

00:29:34.530 --> 00:29:39.310
just in time for the pandemic,
you launched an initiative

00:29:39.310 --> 00:29:42.987
to transform the
secretariat, transform WHO.

00:29:43.840 --> 00:29:46.580
So there's been a lot of,
you know public discussion

00:29:46.580 --> 00:29:49.740
as there is in these
kinds of situations about,

00:29:49.740 --> 00:29:53.743
you know, how the pandemic will
impact WHO's transformation.

00:29:55.020 --> 00:29:57.040
So what are your thoughts on it?

00:29:57.040 --> 00:30:00.110
And as one would think that,

00:30:00.110 --> 00:30:03.570
if it ain't broke, don't fix
it, in terms of certain aspects

00:30:03.570 --> 00:30:07.810
while other aspects might
require some change,

00:30:07.810 --> 00:30:11.933
what is your perspective on
that kind of a discussion?

00:30:13.320 --> 00:30:14.620
- Yeah, thank you.

00:30:14.620 --> 00:30:17.003
I mean, that's a very important question.

00:30:20.135 --> 00:30:21.750
You know, I will take you back actually,

00:30:21.750 --> 00:30:23.820
when I was running for my campaign

00:30:24.730 --> 00:30:27.130
for this position, for DG,

00:30:27.130 --> 00:30:29.430
one of the priorities, four priorities,

00:30:29.430 --> 00:30:33.053
one of them was emergency
preparedness and response.

00:30:34.630 --> 00:30:37.413
And then as soon as I was elected,

00:30:38.460 --> 00:30:41.920
the first major speech I had

00:30:42.780 --> 00:30:45.250
was on pandemic preparedness and response.

00:30:45.250 --> 00:30:48.893
And that was in Columbia
University in September, 2017.

00:30:49.820 --> 00:30:54.143
September, 2017, and I was
asked to choose, you know,

00:30:56.700 --> 00:31:00.450
whatever area I would like to speak about

00:31:00.450 --> 00:31:03.810
and I said pandemic preparedness.

00:31:03.810 --> 00:31:06.550
And I told actually the audience a story

00:31:06.550 --> 00:31:11.550
and how that started in the
U.S. and then in a military camp

00:31:13.230 --> 00:31:17.710
in the West, and then how that traveled

00:31:17.710 --> 00:31:22.350
to the East in New York, you know,

00:31:22.350 --> 00:31:24.930
well even started coming to transmission

00:31:24.930 --> 00:31:28.990
and across the Atlantic to Europe.

00:31:28.990 --> 00:31:31.200
I didn't tell them what it was.

00:31:31.200 --> 00:31:32.700
But that was just telling the story

00:31:32.700 --> 00:31:36.340
and how many people were
killed, and then I told them

00:31:36.340 --> 00:31:40.150
the same thing can happen
now, this happened in 1918,

00:31:40.150 --> 00:31:41.853
but the same thing can happen now.

00:31:43.550 --> 00:31:48.420
Then of course we started the
design of the transformation

00:31:48.420 --> 00:31:50.960
and because it was my election promise.

00:31:50.960 --> 00:31:54.603
So one of the pillars was emergency,

00:31:55.470 --> 00:32:00.470
and we started actually building things

00:32:01.780 --> 00:32:04.130
that are very important
for emergency preparedness

00:32:04.130 --> 00:32:07.180
and response, not only
the talk in Columbia,

00:32:07.180 --> 00:32:11.810
the first major speech I had
as a DG, after I became DG,

00:32:11.810 --> 00:32:15.820
but that was real, our fear was real,

00:32:15.820 --> 00:32:20.570
and that's why I made it
part of the transformation.

00:32:20.570 --> 00:32:24.930
And we established as a
result of the transformation,

00:32:24.930 --> 00:32:29.930
new emergency preparedness
division, just for preparedness.

00:32:30.860 --> 00:32:33.253
And then a new science division,

00:32:34.130 --> 00:32:38.893
with the first ever chief
scientist was established.

00:32:39.850 --> 00:32:44.850
And of course, data, we pulled
all data functions together

00:32:45.180 --> 00:32:50.180
and we articulated with
clarity, some of the changes

00:32:52.230 --> 00:32:55.580
we need to make in order to help

00:32:55.580 --> 00:32:57.760
with emergency preparedness but of course

00:32:57.760 --> 00:32:59.543
the rest of the programs.

00:33:00.789 --> 00:33:05.090
Also WHO Academy, to help us with training

00:33:05.090 --> 00:33:08.610
because you need to build capacity.

00:33:08.610 --> 00:33:11.110
And that's very, very important.

00:33:11.110 --> 00:33:14.740
And of course other things, I
don't want to waste your time

00:33:14.740 --> 00:33:19.240
and the time of our audiences,
students by listing all.

00:33:19.240 --> 00:33:22.340
But most of the things we had done

00:33:24.140 --> 00:33:26.850
as part of our transformation
were very relevant

00:33:26.850 --> 00:33:28.273
for emergency response.

00:33:29.420 --> 00:33:33.363
Actually, we used those
to fight the pandemic.

00:33:34.610 --> 00:33:37.890
Although these things
were done just few months

00:33:37.890 --> 00:33:42.030
before the pandemic, but
they were very helpful

00:33:42.030 --> 00:33:43.460
in many ways.

00:33:43.460 --> 00:33:45.094
I wish we had more time.

00:33:45.094 --> 00:33:46.100
(laughing)

00:33:46.100 --> 00:33:47.253
It was like immediate.

00:33:48.700 --> 00:33:53.700
And now, from what I see
it's a matter of building

00:33:54.730 --> 00:33:55.930
on what we have started.

00:33:57.420 --> 00:34:00.200
And the other very important
element in our transformation

00:34:00.200 --> 00:34:04.810
was to make WHO a learning organization.

00:34:04.810 --> 00:34:09.310
To make change constant,
meaning we will take

00:34:09.310 --> 00:34:11.800
the recommendations from
the different panels,

00:34:11.800 --> 00:34:16.800
from the IPPR, from the IHR
review committee, from IOC,

00:34:17.320 --> 00:34:20.420
from other platforms, even
if you have crazy ideas

00:34:20.420 --> 00:34:22.540
from your university
which I'm really close to,

00:34:22.540 --> 00:34:25.850
give us, this pandemic is unprecedented.

00:34:25.850 --> 00:34:30.850
It has affected everybody,
everybody on earth, every life.

00:34:31.000 --> 00:34:32.750
So we need to listen

00:34:32.750 --> 00:34:36.127
and accommodate that and
continue to change WHO.

00:34:37.170 --> 00:34:40.883
So we have started, why
transformation is important.

00:34:44.340 --> 00:34:46.940
We have started that
transformation journey, I mean,

00:34:48.030 --> 00:34:51.610
and we know why
transformation is important

00:34:51.610 --> 00:34:54.810
but we even know that
change should be a constant

00:34:54.810 --> 00:34:57.350
and we should accommodate change

00:34:57.350 --> 00:35:01.490
and recalibrate and reposition
WHO, of course the whole

00:35:01.490 --> 00:35:05.170
global capacity on regular basis.

00:35:05.170 --> 00:35:07.490
So there is no problem that we started.

00:35:07.490 --> 00:35:10.300
We started something that's helpful

00:35:10.300 --> 00:35:13.700
for emergency preparedness and response.

00:35:13.700 --> 00:35:15.860
It's a matter of building on it

00:35:15.860 --> 00:35:17.520
but we need to accommodate more.

00:35:17.520 --> 00:35:21.520
This is unprecedented, so
we should do even more.

00:35:21.520 --> 00:35:24.810
We should even change
things more significantly

00:35:24.810 --> 00:35:25.863
than it is now.

00:35:28.030 --> 00:35:32.123
- That's wonderful and thanks
for being open to ideas.

00:35:36.391 --> 00:35:40.090
That's a great of looking at
it, sort of how, as a globe,

00:35:40.090 --> 00:35:43.960
we learned from this
experience and just as an aside

00:35:43.960 --> 00:35:47.380
Yale is well represented
in some of these things,

00:35:47.380 --> 00:35:50.760
as you know, professor Ernesto Zedillo,

00:35:50.760 --> 00:35:53.720
former president of
Mexico is a Yale professor

00:35:53.720 --> 00:35:56.070
and is a part of the IPPR.

00:35:56.070 --> 00:35:59.000
So there is, you know, that input.

00:35:59.000 --> 00:36:01.957
So switching to the audience questions

00:36:05.190 --> 00:36:07.630
and we are getting some
very interesting questions.

00:36:07.630 --> 00:36:10.700
And one question, I think you
briefly touched upon that,

00:36:10.700 --> 00:36:12.820
but you might wanna expand on that

00:36:12.820 --> 00:36:15.310
because of the specificity of the question

00:36:15.310 --> 00:36:19.560
and this, you know
questionnaire acknowledges,

00:36:19.560 --> 00:36:22.770
questioner acknowledges that,

00:36:22.770 --> 00:36:27.050
or highlights the disjointed
nature of the global response

00:36:27.050 --> 00:36:30.930
from various countries and other entities,

00:36:30.930 --> 00:36:32.263
private or public.

00:36:34.430 --> 00:36:38.970
Is there one specific
item that jumps to mind

00:36:38.970 --> 00:36:40.660
as the most critical step

00:36:40.660 --> 00:36:42.920
we as a global community could take

00:36:42.920 --> 00:36:46.923
to minimize such
discontinuity in the future?

00:36:48.120 --> 00:36:50.516
- Can you rephrase the question?

00:36:50.516 --> 00:36:54.162
- So, (coughing)

00:36:54.162 --> 00:36:55.830
the premise of the question

00:36:55.830 --> 00:36:59.240
is that there has been some
discontinuity in the response

00:36:59.240 --> 00:37:04.040
by various countries and
private and public entities.

00:37:04.040 --> 00:37:08.660
Is there a single step or
sort of a finite set of steps

00:37:08.660 --> 00:37:11.720
that come to your mind
that would prevent that

00:37:11.720 --> 00:37:15.400
from happening in terms
of a disjointed response

00:37:16.310 --> 00:37:18.170
from happening in future pandemics

00:37:18.170 --> 00:37:19.970
and large public health emergencies?

00:37:22.060 --> 00:37:24.800
- Yeah, so when you say
disjointed, are you talking

00:37:24.800 --> 00:37:28.560
about global or at country level?

00:37:28.560 --> 00:37:31.323
- I'm talking about
global, between countries.

00:37:32.940 --> 00:37:36.730
- So you're mean no
uniformity in response.

00:37:36.730 --> 00:37:41.523
- Exactly, so no uniformity,
so Europe's response,

00:37:42.410 --> 00:37:46.190
not harmonized with the U.S.
response and the response

00:37:46.190 --> 00:37:49.210
and sort of various countries
implementing strategies

00:37:49.210 --> 00:37:52.830
that has have global
implications, even for example

00:37:52.830 --> 00:37:57.830
on supply chains for
vaccines and treatment

00:37:58.720 --> 00:38:01.993
non-pharmaceutical interventions
and so on and so forth.

00:38:03.420 --> 00:38:07.840
And so that fragmentation
perhaps hasn't served us well.

00:38:07.840 --> 00:38:10.360
Is there anything, first of
all, feel free to question

00:38:10.360 --> 00:38:15.360
the premise of this
question, but if you do agree

00:38:15.870 --> 00:38:18.880
that there has been this
fragmented response this time

00:38:18.880 --> 00:38:22.720
by various countries, are there mechanisms

00:38:22.720 --> 00:38:25.423
that come to your mind that
could help prevent that?

00:38:26.550 --> 00:38:28.910
- Yeah, thank you, thank you very much.

00:38:28.910 --> 00:38:32.290
First of all, I don't think
it would be a surprise

00:38:32.290 --> 00:38:34.913
to the students, but
I'll tell you one thing.

00:38:35.750 --> 00:38:37.920
Those who were not listening are actually

00:38:37.920 --> 00:38:39.442
the high-income countries.

00:38:39.442 --> 00:38:40.410
(laughing)

00:38:40.410 --> 00:38:42.530
Because they think that they have

00:38:42.530 --> 00:38:44.780
the best system on earth.

00:38:44.780 --> 00:38:46.180
So they don't listen to WHO.

00:38:47.950 --> 00:38:52.950
But WHO has experts that come
from different countries.

00:38:54.680 --> 00:38:59.450
So no country can claim that
it has better capacity than WHO

00:38:59.450 --> 00:39:02.950
because we are a collection
of diverse experiences

00:39:02.950 --> 00:39:07.950
and capacities that could be
better than any single country,

00:39:09.690 --> 00:39:13.320
and advice from WHO is
like a collective advice

00:39:13.320 --> 00:39:17.750
from a center that
accommodates all the experience

00:39:17.750 --> 00:39:19.523
and practices of many countries.

00:39:20.550 --> 00:39:25.550
So, I think that's one,

00:39:25.600 --> 00:39:27.990
meaning, the high-income countries

00:39:27.990 --> 00:39:30.270
should take WHO seriously.

00:39:30.270 --> 00:39:34.750
And when WHO advises, they should listen.

00:39:34.750 --> 00:39:39.050
You may remember, some high
income countries when we said

00:39:39.050 --> 00:39:43.677
this virus is very
dangerous, they were saying,

00:39:43.677 --> 00:39:46.570
"Oh, it's flu, we can manage it."

00:39:46.570 --> 00:39:50.300
When we say this virus is
Public Enemy Number One,

00:39:50.300 --> 00:39:52.573
in February last year, maybe you remember,

00:39:53.590 --> 00:39:56.637
they were saying, "No, no,
no, we can let it circulate.

00:39:56.637 --> 00:39:58.847
"and then there could be herd immunity

00:39:58.847 --> 00:40:03.497
"by letting it circulate freely,
and then we will be okay."

00:40:05.622 --> 00:40:09.407
We fought back saying,
"No, it's not a flu.

00:40:09.407 --> 00:40:14.007
"It's a very dangerous virus,
it's very transmittable,

00:40:14.007 --> 00:40:15.437
"it's a killer.

00:40:15.437 --> 00:40:17.817
"It has these two dangerous combinations,

00:40:17.817 --> 00:40:20.870
"that's why we call it
public enemy number one."

00:40:20.870 --> 00:40:25.250
And sadly the high income countries

00:40:25.250 --> 00:40:28.010
were not taking it seriously
because they believe

00:40:28.010 --> 00:40:31.330
that they have better capacity.

00:40:31.330 --> 00:40:33.043
That's very important by the way.

00:40:35.220 --> 00:40:38.760
And then of course, even
if you don't categorize

00:40:38.760 --> 00:40:41.010
by high, middle and low income countries,

00:40:41.010 --> 00:40:43.720
the many countries because
of many other reasons

00:40:43.720 --> 00:40:47.460
that they were not following the advisers.

00:40:47.460 --> 00:40:52.460
So going forward, what do we
do to address this problem?

00:40:52.500 --> 00:40:54.603
That's why we're saying pandemic treaty.

00:40:55.870 --> 00:41:00.870
There will be areas that
will address, you know,

00:41:04.499 --> 00:41:09.499
the important elements that the country

00:41:09.770 --> 00:41:13.920
should really observe, all
countries whether they are high,

00:41:13.920 --> 00:41:17.630
middle, or low income countries,

00:41:17.630 --> 00:41:19.700
and accommodating it
in the pandemic treaty

00:41:19.700 --> 00:41:22.510
would be very, very important.

00:41:22.510 --> 00:41:26.143
But even in the absence of
the pandemic treaty now,

00:41:27.010 --> 00:41:31.293
uniformity of action or
response is very important.

00:41:32.200 --> 00:41:37.160
Because when there is uniformity,
and when all countries

00:41:37.160 --> 00:41:40.450
do the same thing at the same time,

00:41:40.450 --> 00:41:43.963
the virus will not get
a space to maneuver.

00:41:44.840 --> 00:41:47.323
And very simple and
anybody can understand it.

00:41:49.510 --> 00:41:54.510
You deny the virus a space to
maneuver when you act as one.

00:41:55.640 --> 00:41:58.540
That's why we have been
calling for cooperation,

00:41:58.540 --> 00:42:01.580
that's why we have been
asking for solidarity.

00:42:01.580 --> 00:42:05.760
We have been proposing
actions, public health,

00:42:05.760 --> 00:42:08.393
and we have been saying, please do it all,

00:42:10.290 --> 00:42:15.290
because we knew that the
same action everywhere

00:42:15.440 --> 00:42:20.100
could actually stop or slow the virus.

00:42:20.100 --> 00:42:23.820
So going forward, I
think institutionalizing

00:42:23.820 --> 00:42:26.090
the solutions will be important.

00:42:26.090 --> 00:42:28.090
And the pandemic treaty will be one

00:42:28.090 --> 00:42:32.840
that will institutionalize,
and then of course,

00:42:32.840 --> 00:42:37.780
both high-income and
low-income or all categories,

00:42:37.780 --> 00:42:41.560
high, middle, upper middle, low income,

00:42:41.560 --> 00:42:45.437
should listen to a multi-lateral
organization like WHO.

00:42:47.450 --> 00:42:51.250
We have been consistent
on this as you know.

00:42:51.250 --> 00:42:54.060
Although the virus was new
and there could be some things

00:42:54.060 --> 00:42:57.247
we didn't know, we started by
saying, "We don't know this,

00:42:57.247 --> 00:42:59.820
"but this is what we think you should do."

00:42:59.820 --> 00:43:02.420
And then we started to learn together

00:43:02.420 --> 00:43:06.820
and we know better now and going forward

00:43:06.820 --> 00:43:10.423
I think that uniform action
will be very, very important.

00:43:12.830 --> 00:43:14.530
Back to you Saad.

00:43:14.530 --> 00:43:16.250
- Yeah, no, thank you.

00:43:16.250 --> 00:43:19.050
And so there's another
question from the audience

00:43:20.010 --> 00:43:25.010
about the fact that global
health funding has been reactive

00:43:26.300 --> 00:43:30.450
rather than sustained and sustainable.

00:43:30.450 --> 00:43:34.300
So, and you very nicely
sort of talked about

00:43:34.300 --> 00:43:38.650
learning from crises to move forward.

00:43:38.650 --> 00:43:42.340
So how do we ensure going forward

00:43:42.340 --> 00:43:45.380
that governments and
donors are held accountable

00:43:45.380 --> 00:43:49.113
to long-term impactful
funding for health equity?

00:43:51.510 --> 00:43:53.090
- Yeah, and thank you so much.

00:43:53.090 --> 00:43:56.130
I mean, if we are going
to address that problem,

00:43:56.130 --> 00:43:58.723
that problem is very visible as you said,

00:44:00.210 --> 00:44:05.210
we have to get out of this
panic and neglect cycle.

00:44:07.430 --> 00:44:10.800
So we always move in a
vicious cycle, you know,

00:44:10.800 --> 00:44:13.660
panic, neglect, panic, neglect, okay?

00:44:13.660 --> 00:44:16.660
There is an epidemic or outbreak,

00:44:16.660 --> 00:44:20.200
we panic, we react, we finance,

00:44:20.200 --> 00:44:22.520
and then as soon as that's taken care of

00:44:22.520 --> 00:44:25.780
then we neglect and so on.

00:44:25.780 --> 00:44:29.810
So we have to recognize health threats

00:44:29.810 --> 00:44:31.323
as a cornerstone threat.

00:44:32.270 --> 00:44:34.900
And the same thing can happen anytime.

00:44:34.900 --> 00:44:38.823
So the world should be
prepared anytime, at all times.

00:44:40.180 --> 00:44:43.720
Treat it like for instance
a security problem.

00:44:43.720 --> 00:44:47.960
Countries, you know, the budgets they have

00:44:47.960 --> 00:44:52.600
for their defense or security,

00:44:52.600 --> 00:44:55.233
it's big and it's sustainable.

00:44:56.410 --> 00:45:00.770
And I think they have
seen how health actually

00:45:00.770 --> 00:45:05.770
impacts even more than a terrorist
because they prepare that

00:45:07.010 --> 00:45:09.110
because maybe because they have a threat

00:45:09.110 --> 00:45:11.770
from their neighbors or beyond,

00:45:11.770 --> 00:45:15.883
they prepare because of fear of terrorism,

00:45:17.370 --> 00:45:22.370
but the virus I think has
shown that it has more impact

00:45:24.700 --> 00:45:26.343
than what we fear.

00:45:28.050 --> 00:45:33.050
It has impacted our economy,
it has impacted us politically,

00:45:33.930 --> 00:45:35.833
socially, you name it.

00:45:37.630 --> 00:45:41.993
And the whole world has
been taken actually hostage.

00:45:43.180 --> 00:45:46.310
I have never seen since
the second world war

00:45:46.310 --> 00:45:51.060
if there is anything that has
really frozen the whole world.

00:45:51.060 --> 00:45:53.800
Even second world war
some parts were okay.

00:45:56.370 --> 00:45:59.863
So it affected everything,

00:46:01.100 --> 00:46:05.673
and it has shown that health is centered.

00:46:07.470 --> 00:46:12.420
So if we cannot finance it sustainably,

00:46:12.420 --> 00:46:15.453
then I think there is
something wrong with us.

00:46:16.360 --> 00:46:17.593
We are not listening.

00:46:18.600 --> 00:46:21.400
I think it's enough to listen now.

00:46:21.400 --> 00:46:22.820
More than enough.

00:46:22.820 --> 00:46:25.833
We were affected the hard way.

00:46:26.790 --> 00:46:31.790
So I think we have listened,
and that's what I believe,

00:46:31.920 --> 00:46:34.373
and there should be a
significant investment.

00:46:35.470 --> 00:46:37.713
And for sustainability of funding,

00:46:38.610 --> 00:46:41.910
where I would have start
is, if we realize that

00:46:41.910 --> 00:46:45.160
health is central and
we don't want anything

00:46:45.160 --> 00:46:47.940
like this to happen,
then the simple answer

00:46:47.940 --> 00:46:52.700
is we have to believe in
universal health coverage.

00:46:52.700 --> 00:46:55.663
We should stop commercializing health.

00:46:57.070 --> 00:47:00.063
Health is it not a commodity for sale.

00:47:01.900 --> 00:47:06.900
Health should it be taken
as a human rights issue.

00:47:08.490 --> 00:47:09.723
Like a foundation.

00:47:12.580 --> 00:47:17.340
And one ask I have is,
each and every country

00:47:17.340 --> 00:47:19.830
to put it in its constitution

00:47:19.830 --> 00:47:22.193
as a fundamental human rights issue.

00:47:24.460 --> 00:47:26.223
And commit to health for all,

00:47:27.210 --> 00:47:30.500
and do whatever, even
the poorest on earth,

00:47:30.500 --> 00:47:35.500
whatever to keep its people healthy,

00:47:36.320 --> 00:47:38.443
to strengthen its system.

00:47:40.000 --> 00:47:44.870
You know, what UK did because I just want,

00:47:44.870 --> 00:47:48.533
I don't want any country
to have any excuses.

00:47:49.750 --> 00:47:53.910
The UK decided on NHS immediately after

00:47:53.910 --> 00:47:55.313
the Second World War.

00:47:56.720 --> 00:48:00.753
Lord Beveridge, when its
economy was in tatters,

00:48:03.100 --> 00:48:06.247
it decided to have health
for all, that's NHS.

00:48:07.240 --> 00:48:09.713
So no country can have any excuse.

00:48:10.650 --> 00:48:14.430
So if we want this not to happen again

00:48:14.430 --> 00:48:18.433
I don't see it as financing
only, and meaning finance it,

00:48:19.550 --> 00:48:21.800
then I think that level of commitment

00:48:21.800 --> 00:48:23.480
will be very important.

00:48:23.480 --> 00:48:25.970
Health is central, health for all,

00:48:25.970 --> 00:48:28.640
we should move toward this health for all,

00:48:28.640 --> 00:48:31.530
and take it, even recognize
it in our constitution.

00:48:31.530 --> 00:48:33.700
Many countries have done that,

00:48:33.700 --> 00:48:36.520
and all countries should do that.

00:48:36.520 --> 00:48:39.540
And they shouldn't commercialize health.

00:48:39.540 --> 00:48:41.540
When I say this, by the
way I have to be clear.

00:48:41.540 --> 00:48:43.730
I'm not saying private
sector should not be involved

00:48:43.730 --> 00:48:45.530
and everything should be public,

00:48:45.530 --> 00:48:48.250
what I'm saying is government
should take responsibility

00:48:48.250 --> 00:48:51.110
and the services can be
provided through government,

00:48:51.110 --> 00:48:56.110
public or private sector,
or what do you call it,

00:48:56.490 --> 00:49:00.230
a hybrid or one of
them, it doesn't matter,

00:49:00.230 --> 00:49:01.880
it's up to the country to choose.

00:49:02.800 --> 00:49:06.970
But I think the government
spending, I mean, contribution

00:49:06.970 --> 00:49:09.000
is very, very important and government

00:49:09.000 --> 00:49:13.693
should take responsibility
to make this happen.

00:49:14.650 --> 00:49:16.243
So that's how we can address

00:49:16.243 --> 00:49:19.900
the sustainability of financing.

00:49:19.900 --> 00:49:23.580
Then globally, if we do this
locally at national level,

00:49:23.580 --> 00:49:27.030
then sustaining global
efforts will not be a problem.

00:49:27.030 --> 00:49:29.520
And for that, the recommendations we have

00:49:29.520 --> 00:49:32.420
is global financing.

00:49:32.420 --> 00:49:36.713
Let's not try to have new
institutions for global financing.

00:49:37.890 --> 00:49:39.443
Even for local, by the way.

00:49:40.730 --> 00:49:43.670
We can use IMF, we can use The World Bank,

00:49:43.670 --> 00:49:46.960
we can use the regional banks,
of course they should develop

00:49:50.170 --> 00:49:55.170
some flexible processes,
very agile processes,

00:49:57.950 --> 00:50:01.120
and then commitment to pandemic
preparedness and response,

00:50:01.120 --> 00:50:04.123
but the existing institutions can do it.

00:50:05.040 --> 00:50:07.110
Thank you, sorry, I
took a long on this one

00:50:07.110 --> 00:50:08.877
because it's very important.

00:50:08.877 --> 00:50:11.130
- No, I agree and it's nuanced.

00:50:11.130 --> 00:50:16.130
So as we reach sort of the
last part of our conversation,

00:50:17.240 --> 00:50:20.763
so I wanna highlight another
question from the audience.

00:50:22.060 --> 00:50:23.680
As you know that in pandemics

00:50:23.680 --> 00:50:25.330
and large public health emergencies

00:50:25.330 --> 00:50:27.150
the evidence base is changing.

00:50:27.150 --> 00:50:31.320
So the question is, is there
a way, is there a creative way

00:50:31.320 --> 00:50:33.990
for global universities to
be more directly helpful

00:50:33.990 --> 00:50:38.850
to the WHO when its needs are
acute for all evidence-based

00:50:38.850 --> 00:50:40.820
reviews and rapid responses?

00:50:40.820 --> 00:50:43.920
You know, the collaborating
center model is very much there

00:50:43.920 --> 00:50:45.890
but as you know, that it takes time

00:50:45.890 --> 00:50:49.640
and often new players are
needed and agility is needed

00:50:49.640 --> 00:50:51.540
in public health emergencies.

00:50:51.540 --> 00:50:53.290
So is there, you know, for example,

00:50:53.290 --> 00:50:55.620
the questionnaire suggests,
you know, is there a value

00:50:55.620 --> 00:51:00.620
of a master contract that
doesn't involve a lot of money

00:51:03.440 --> 00:51:06.460
or perhaps even no money
but there is a framework

00:51:06.460 --> 00:51:08.910
for rapid synthesis of evidence,

00:51:08.910 --> 00:51:11.550
so the intellectual
capacity that is out there

00:51:11.550 --> 00:51:13.740
can be fully marshaled.

00:51:13.740 --> 00:51:15.910
So we'd love to hear
your thoughts on that,

00:51:15.910 --> 00:51:19.950
especially as you know,
one of the agenda you have

00:51:19.950 --> 00:51:23.370
within WHO is the WHO Academy
so this capacity building,

00:51:23.370 --> 00:51:27.130
this focus on evidence, is I think in line

00:51:27.130 --> 00:51:30.223
with this kind of question
in a potential proposal.

00:51:32.150 --> 00:51:34.210
- Thank you, I think you read my mind.

00:51:34.210 --> 00:51:37.370
I was going to say, "Oh, we
have collaborating centers,"

00:51:37.370 --> 00:51:39.947
but you preempted by saying,

00:51:39.947 --> 00:51:42.194
"I know about collaborating centers, so."

00:51:42.194 --> 00:51:43.990
(laughing)

00:51:43.990 --> 00:51:48.170
This is by the way, we
believe in networks.

00:51:48.170 --> 00:51:50.240
We have several networks there're,

00:51:50.240 --> 00:51:51.500
some of them university based,

00:51:51.500 --> 00:51:54.513
some of them, other institutional based,

00:51:55.910 --> 00:52:00.380
but from the way you
said, the master contract,

00:52:00.380 --> 00:52:04.680
it doesn't mean it involves
money of universities.

00:52:04.680 --> 00:52:07.773
I think network of those
would be very important.

00:52:08.880 --> 00:52:11.623
Why not you develop this as a concept.

00:52:12.960 --> 00:52:15.440
What I like about Yale
is the grand strategy,

00:52:15.440 --> 00:52:19.490
that's where you process
and bring new ideas.

00:52:19.490 --> 00:52:24.490
So I see this as a new idea
with just purely universities.

00:52:25.480 --> 00:52:30.140
And we don't go into that
process of collaborating centers

00:52:30.140 --> 00:52:33.450
but a different way of
partnership with universities,

00:52:33.450 --> 00:52:37.400
both in the South and North,
everywhere all over the world.

00:52:37.400 --> 00:52:40.480
Low income, high income, middle income.

00:52:40.480 --> 00:52:43.620
And if you can develop a
concept with your students

00:52:43.620 --> 00:52:46.580
and we would be happy to have
a followup discussion on this

00:52:46.580 --> 00:52:51.580
but I think this may evolve as a good way

00:52:53.370 --> 00:52:55.307
of partnership with WHO.

00:52:56.270 --> 00:52:58.053
Thank you so much for that.

00:52:58.053 --> 00:53:00.130
- Wonderful, so the last question--

00:53:00.130 --> 00:53:02.787
- And then we have the WHO Academy,

00:53:02.787 --> 00:53:04.890
but the partnership with Universities

00:53:04.890 --> 00:53:07.560
may not be just for training.

00:53:07.560 --> 00:53:10.910
It could be other things, that's why

00:53:10.910 --> 00:53:13.740
it could be more than training.

00:53:13.740 --> 00:53:16.040
Of course the WHO Academy
starts with training

00:53:16.040 --> 00:53:20.650
but for the future, it
will have a research arm.

00:53:20.650 --> 00:53:24.790
So that could be the center, by the way,

00:53:24.790 --> 00:53:27.810
to coordinate all the opportunities

00:53:27.810 --> 00:53:30.260
that we can have globally.

00:53:30.260 --> 00:53:32.940
By the way, the WHO Academy is also new.

00:53:32.940 --> 00:53:35.420
It's an outcome of the transformation

00:53:36.870 --> 00:53:40.440
and it can also take part in here.

00:53:40.440 --> 00:53:42.840
But that's a very good
idea, I would be happy

00:53:42.840 --> 00:53:46.073
if you can develop it further
and have a follow-up meeting.

00:53:47.160 --> 00:53:51.400
- So, one last question
before we wrap up is,

00:53:51.400 --> 00:53:53.410
that as we move beyond the pandemic

00:53:53.410 --> 00:53:56.830
and you have vociferously
advocated from the beginning,

00:53:56.830 --> 00:53:59.480
even before you took charge of WHO

00:53:59.480 --> 00:54:02.023
about the universal health care agenda.

00:54:02.900 --> 00:54:07.460
How do we, so the pandemic will
subside one way or another,

00:54:07.460 --> 00:54:09.740
we will have to be ready for the next one.

00:54:09.740 --> 00:54:14.740
How do we reengage or even
engage with more vigor

00:54:16.080 --> 00:54:21.033
in the universal health care
agenda in the next few years?

00:54:22.900 --> 00:54:27.900
- Yeah, I think this is
very, very important.

00:54:31.074 --> 00:54:36.074
We are already saying to
the international community

00:54:36.370 --> 00:54:40.790
what I just said earlier, and
now, because of this pandemic,

00:54:40.790 --> 00:54:43.347
we know how central health is.

00:54:44.500 --> 00:54:48.148
And that investing in
universal health coverage

00:54:48.148 --> 00:54:51.363
is the answer.

00:54:52.340 --> 00:54:57.340
And the pandemic itself has
already, what do you call it,

00:55:00.330 --> 00:55:05.330
strengthened the case for
health for all, or UHC.

00:55:07.830 --> 00:55:11.300
So we have already started engaging

00:55:11.300 --> 00:55:14.630
but we will continue to even do it more.

00:55:14.630 --> 00:55:18.550
But the messages are exactly
what I told you earlier,

00:55:18.550 --> 00:55:19.763
what I said earlier.

00:55:21.120 --> 00:55:25.160
Meaning if you ask me one
thing, very concrete thing

00:55:25.160 --> 00:55:29.920
that countries can do, if they can put it,

00:55:29.920 --> 00:55:32.890
their commitment could be
put in their constitution,

00:55:32.890 --> 00:55:37.890
UHC or health for all.

00:55:38.320 --> 00:55:42.160
So we have a specific ask now to make,

00:55:42.160 --> 00:55:44.800
and to ask countries when we engage,

00:55:44.800 --> 00:55:47.900
when we continue to engage
that they should put it

00:55:47.900 --> 00:55:51.580
in their constitution
because that's actually

00:55:54.400 --> 00:55:59.400
how you would know how
committed a country is

00:55:59.680 --> 00:56:02.990
when they put it in their constitution.

00:56:02.990 --> 00:56:07.990
So it made it easier for
us now to explain why,

00:56:10.890 --> 00:56:11.863
health is central.

00:56:13.310 --> 00:56:15.910
It's very tragic, all that happened,

00:56:15.910 --> 00:56:19.123
more than 3.2 million
people have died already,

00:56:20.360 --> 00:56:24.400
because of this pandemic,
but it has shown us

00:56:24.400 --> 00:56:29.400
beyond any doubt, the
centrality of health.

00:56:29.480 --> 00:56:33.220
So there is already good
opportunity to push the agenda

00:56:33.220 --> 00:56:35.630
and look forward to working with you,

00:56:35.630 --> 00:56:37.100
to push the health for all,

00:56:37.100 --> 00:56:39.713
or the universal health coverage agenda.

00:56:40.620 --> 00:56:44.220
- Thank you very much, and as
we wrap up this conversation

00:56:44.220 --> 00:56:47.020
I wanna thank you, Dr. Tedros

00:56:47.020 --> 00:56:50.950
for being a passionate advocate
for universal health care

00:56:50.950 --> 00:56:55.900
and very specifically health equity for

00:56:55.900 --> 00:56:58.500
well before this pandemic, and I know

00:56:58.500 --> 00:57:01.540
based on that track record
that you will continue to be

00:57:01.540 --> 00:57:05.120
an advocate for global health equity.

00:57:05.120 --> 00:57:08.250
And thank you for working
with the fierce urgency of now

00:57:08.250 --> 00:57:10.960
to paraphrase, to borrow a phrase

00:57:10.960 --> 00:57:14.660
from Martin Luther King Jr, that you know,

00:57:14.660 --> 00:57:16.930
which was required in this pandemic.

00:57:16.930 --> 00:57:21.930
And I think WHO's response, the
global response has shown us

00:57:24.150 --> 00:57:29.150
the indispensable nature
of institutions like WHO,

00:57:30.580 --> 00:57:33.320
and that these institutions matter.

00:57:33.320 --> 00:57:38.320
It matter from Kansas to Katmandu,

00:57:39.420 --> 00:57:43.240
because we all use one or another function

00:57:43.240 --> 00:57:47.430
that WHO fulfills, the convening function

00:57:47.430 --> 00:57:52.330
or the technical guidance
function benefits us all,

00:57:52.330 --> 00:57:54.700
and it is in our interest to make sure

00:57:54.700 --> 00:57:58.470
that these institutions and
specifically WHO is healthy

00:57:58.470 --> 00:58:02.000
and continues to move forward.

00:58:02.000 --> 00:58:04.150
So thank you very much
for taking the time,

00:58:04.985 --> 00:58:07.122
we really appreciate you being here today.

00:58:07.122 --> 00:58:09.760
- Thank you, thank you so much, Saad,

00:58:09.760 --> 00:58:13.160
and I would appreciate it if
you can also pass my greetings

00:58:13.160 --> 00:58:15.160
to John Gaddis, eh?

00:58:15.160 --> 00:58:16.460
- I will, absolutely.

00:58:16.460 --> 00:58:19.300
- I hope you will find ways to (mumbles)

00:58:19.300 --> 00:58:20.554
you can catch him, yeah?

00:58:20.554 --> 00:58:23.450
(both laughing)

00:58:23.450 --> 00:58:26.713
So, thank you, thank you so much, bye-bye.

